\\ni^5 &= i \\cdot i^4 = i \\cdot 1 = i, \\\\\ni^6 &= i \\cdot i^5 = i \\cdot i = i^2 = -1, \\\\\ni^7 &= i \\cdot i^6 = i \\cdot (-1) = -i, \\\\\ni^8 &= i \\cdot i^7 = i \\cdot (-i) = i^2 = -1, \\\\\n&\\vdots\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIn general, \\(i^n = i^{n \\mod 4}\\). We can use this property to simplify the given expression.\n\nFirst, simplify \\(i^5\\):\n\\[\ni^5 = i^{5 \\mod 4} = i^1 = i.\n\\]\n\nNext, simplify \\(i^{-25}\\). Note that \\(i^{-25} = \\frac{1}{i^{25}}\\). We can find \\(i^{25}\\) by noting that \\(25 \\mod 4 = 1\\), so:\n\\[\ni^{25} = i^1 = i.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\ni^{-25} = \\frac{1}{i} = \\frac{1}{i} \\cdot \\frac{i}{i} = \\frac{i}{i^2} = \\frac{i}{-1} = -i.\n\\]\n\nFinally, simplify \\(i^{45}\\). We have \\(45 \\mod 4 = 1\\), so:\n\\[\ni^{45} = i^1 = i.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these simplified values back into the original expression:\n\\[\ni^5 + i^{-25} + i^{45} = i + (-i) + i = i - i + i = i.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{i}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{i}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:3"}
